-### [Marching Cubes with Disjoint Patch Decomposition](5004-marching-cubes-with-disjoint-patch-decomposition.md)
-A sibling method that resolves ambiguities by decomposing cells into disjoint patches. It differs by not using Hermite data or QEF minimization, relying instead on topological decomposition for manifold guarantees. Choose when Hermite data is unavailable or when simpler topology handling is preferred.
+*   **Singular QEF:** If all normals associated with a vertex are parallel, the matrix $A_v$ is singular. Use a pseudo-inverse or fallback to the centroid of intersection points [G].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** Edges on the grid boundary have fewer than four adjacent cells. Handle by clamping, mirroring, or omitting faces [G].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If Hermite data is missing for an edge, the method cannot place the vertex accurately. Use midpoint as fallback [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, assuming constant-time lookup and QEF solution (3x3 linear system) [G].
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for grid storage and output mesh [G].
--   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. [G]
--   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with grid resolution [G].
--   **Strengths:** Produces a manifold mesh; reproduces sharp features using Hermite data; robust on uniform grids [S1][S2, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Requires Hermite data (normals) on edges; may produce distorted geometry if Hermite data is inaccurate; outputs quadrilateral faces [S2, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** When a manifold mesh with sharp features is required from uniform grid data with available normals [S1][S2, unofficial].
--   **When Not to Use:** Not given in the sources. [G]
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of grid cells, assuming constant-time lookup and small fixed-size QEF systems (typically 3x3).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing grid data, edge data, and vertex positions.
+*   **Strengths:** Always produces a manifold surface; reproduces sharp features; robust on uniform grids.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires Hermite data; quadrilateral output may require triangulation for some renderers; more complex than standard Marching Cubes.
+*   **When to use:** When sharp features must be preserved and manifold topology is required, especially on uniform grids where standard Dual Contouring fails.
+*   **When not to use:** If Hermite data is unavailable or if triangular output is strictly required without post-processing.
 
 ## Background
-Hermite Dual Marching Cubes was introduced by Nielson as an extension of Dual Marching Cubes to incorporate Hermite data for sharp feature preservation [S1][S2, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of standard Dual Contouring, which can produce non-manifold topology on uniform grids, and standard Marching Cubes, which smooths sharp features [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method is discussed in the context of manifold dual contouring and adaptive contouring [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Hermite Dual Marching Cubes was introduced as an extension to Nielson's Dual Marching Cubes algorithm. It addresses the limitations of standard Dual Contouring, which produces non-manifold surfaces on uniform grids for ambiguous sign configurations, and standard Marching Cubes, which fails to reproduce sharp features. The method was developed to provide a robust, manifold-preserving contouring algorithm that leverages Hermite data for feature preservation.
 
